---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { INSTRUMENTS, OPEN_COUNT, BASE } from "../data/instruments.js";
---

<Page
  title="Analytical instruments for open research"
  description="A studio that builds the pipelines, models, evaluation and interactive instruments for researchers who already have a finding. The interpretation stays theirs."
  current="/"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Open data · Indonesia · instruments, not dashboards</p>
    <h1>Measured carefully.<br /><em>Not yet explained.</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Ten analytical instruments on Indonesian open data: pipelines, models, and
      evaluation against baselines that already contain the easy knowledge. Each one is
      complete as measurement and deliberately short of an explanation — which is the part
      that needs someone who knows the subject.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="cta" href="#instruments">{OPEN_COUNT} instruments seeking an author</a>
      <a class="cta ghost" href="/contribute/">How this works</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Why<span>the gap this fills</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Journals publish figures. Nobody publishes instruments.</h2>
      <p>
        A paper's analysis is usually its most reusable part and its least accessible.
        The figures are flattened into a PDF, the pipeline lives on one laptop, and the
        question a reader actually wants to ask — <em>what happens if I change this
        assumption?</em> — cannot be asked at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Repositories archive the code. Notebook hosts run it. Neither builds the thing a
        non-specialist can use, and no journal will host it for you. That is the gap.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p><b>So the work is split by what it takes, not by who owns it.</b> Measurement
        needs statistics, models and software. Explanation needs the subject. Both get
        named credit for what they did, and neither has a veto over the other.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">How<span>what each side brings</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>How a case comes together</h2>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Method.</b> Which estimator, which baseline, what would count as
                working — decided and written down before anything runs</td>
              <td><b>The question,</b> and why it matters where it is asked</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Models.</b> Built, tuned and scored — statistical or machine-learned,
                whichever the problem actually needs</td>
              <td><b>Domain knowledge.</b> What a result means, what would be surprising,
                what the literature already settled</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Evaluation.</b> Against naive rivals that already contain the easy
                knowledge, with every gate published including the failures</td>
              <td><b>Plausible mechanism.</b> Why the relationship would exist, or why it
                would not</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The instrument.</b> Pipeline, dashboard, figures, all re-runnable</td>
              <td><b>The interpretation,</b> the written discussion, and accountability for
                the finding</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Credit follows the work with <a href="/contribute/#credit">CRediT roles</a>, so
        nobody has to pretend: Methodology, Formal analysis, Validation, Software, Data
        curation and Visualization on one side of that table; Conceptualization,
        Investigation and Writing on the other. Whoever did a thing is credited for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the instruments published so far, the measurement column is done and the
        explanation column is empty. Both halves are open to anyone who can fill them —
        a domain researcher most obviously, but equally someone who can improve a model or
        break an evaluation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="who">
    <div class="rail">Who you are<span>the terms differ</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <h2>Two ways in, and they are not the same offer</h2>
      <p>
        A domain researcher and someone who builds models are being asked for different
        things and getting different things back. Rather than write one page that assumes
        which you are, pick.
      </p>

      <fieldset class="roles">
        <legend class="sr-only">Choose how you would take part</legend>
        <input type="radio" name="role" id="role-researcher" checked />
        <label for="role-researcher">I know a subject</label>
        <input type="radio" name="role" id="role-builder" />
        <label for="role-builder">I build models and software</label>

        <div class="panel panel-researcher">
          <p class="panel-intro">
            You have a finding, a dataset you understand, or a question nobody has measured
            properly. The measurement gets built for you.
          </p>
          <div class="sides">
            <div class="side good">
              <h3>What you get</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>A <b>working instrument</b> for your subject — pipeline, models,
                  evaluation, dashboard — built free</li>
                <li><b>First author.</b> The finding and its interpretation are yours</li>
                <li>You <b>keep copyright</b> and can republish anywhere</li>
                <li>A <b>DOI</b> on request — archived independently, citable, versioned</li>
                <li>Thresholds fixed before results, so the evaluation is defensible to a
                  reviewer</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="side warn">
              <h3>What you should know</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><b>Not peer reviewed.</b> Gates are checking, not refereeing</li>
                <li>This site has <b>no audience yet.</b> You are betting it gets one</li>
                <li>Publishing here before a journal makes it a <b>preprint</b> — check your
                  journal's policy first</li>
                <li><b>Failed gates are published.</b> If the measurement contradicts your
                  finding, the page says so</li>
                <li>Implementation is <b>AI-assisted</b>, disclosed on every instrument</li>
                <li>One person, one server, no grant. <b>It could stop.</b></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="panel-next">
            The specific gap on each instrument is written on its card below.
            <a href="/contribute/">The longer version</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="panel panel-builder">
          <p class="panel-intro">
            You do not need a subject or a finding. The most useful thing here takes a day
            and is the thing this site's credibility actually rests on.
          </p>
          <div class="tw">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>What you do</th><th>Effort</th><th>Credit</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>Break an evaluation.</b> Wrong metric for the problem? Did a
                    baseline see the test set? Does the cross-validation actually prevent
                    leakage? If you succeed, that gets published</td>
                  <td>a day</td><td>Validation</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>Beat a baseline.</b> Build a stronger rival than the one an
                    instrument was scored against. Win or lose, the result is worth
                    publishing</td>
                  <td>a week</td><td>Formal analysis</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>Build an instrument.</b> Pipeline, models, gates and dashboard for
                    someone's finding, to the <a href="/standards/">published standard</a></td>
                  <td>weeks</td><td>Software · Methodology · Visualization</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="sides">
            <div class="side good">
              <h3>What you get</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><b>Named credit</b> on a public artifact, by CRediT role rather than a
                  thank-you line</li>
                <li>A portfolio piece where <b>the validation is visible</b> — rarer than it
                  should be</li>
                <li>Your code stays yours, permissively licensed</li>
                <li>An attack that succeeds is <b>published as a finding</b>, not quietly
                  absorbed</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="side warn">
              <h3>What you should know</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><b>Thresholds are pre-registered and do not move.</b> If your model
                  loses to a naive baseline, that is what publishes</li>
                <li><b>No payment</b> by default. Paid work is separate and offered
                  case by case</li>
                <li>Existing implementation is <b>AI-assisted</b> — you may be reviewing code
                  a model wrote under direction</li>
                <li>Capacity is one person's attention. Review is <b>slow</b></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="panel-next">
            Three openings right now: <b>transit-equity</b> has two failing hard gates — are
            they fatal or cosmetic? <b>rice-security</b>'s R² goes −11.09 to 0.82 through OLS
            calibration — legitimate, or fitting the answer? <b>fire-haze</b> scores a 3.8%
            event class with ROC-AUC — is precision-recall the honest metric?
            <a href="/contribute/">The longer version</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </fieldset>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="instruments">
    <div class="rail">Instruments<span>{OPEN_COUNT} seeking an author</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <h2>Ten instruments, each missing one thing</h2>
      <p>
        Every one is complete as measurement: the pipeline runs, the gates are published
        including the failures, the dashboard works. None has an explanation yet, and that
        is a gap rather than an omission — it needs knowledge none of these instruments
        contain.
      </p>

      <ul class="cards">
        {INSTRUMENTS.map((i) => (
          <li class="card">
            <div class="card-head">
              <a class="card-title" href={`${BASE}/${i.slug}/`}>
                {i.title} <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              </a>
              <span class="chip">seeking author</span>
            </div>
            <p class="card-line">{i.line}</p>
            <p class="card-found"><b>What it found.</b> {i.found}</p>
            <p class="card-gap"><b>What is missing.</b> {i.gap}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          If one of these is your subject, the measurement is already done. Writing the
          explanation earns <b>first author</b>; the measurement earns <b>methods
          co-author</b>. If instead you can see something wrong with how one was measured,
          that is a contribution too — see <a href="/contribute/">how to contribute</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="dim">Read <a href="/contribute/">how to contribute</a> first — including the parts that might put you off.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Engineers<span>a smaller ask</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>You do not have to build one to take part</h2>
      <p>
        The most useful contribution an engineer can make here takes a day, and it is the
        one this site depends on most.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Contribution</th><th>Effort</th><th>Credit</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Break it.</b> Attack the evaluation. If you succeed, that gets published</td>
              <td>a day</td><td>Validation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Beat the baseline.</b> Build a stronger rival than the one it was scored against</td>
              <td>a week</td><td>Formal analysis</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Build one.</b> A whole instrument for a researcher's finding</td>
              <td>weeks</td><td>Software, Visualization</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Three specific openings, today: <b>transit-equity</b> has two failing hard gates —
        are they fatal or cosmetic? <b>rice-security</b>'s R² goes −11.09 to 0.82 through
        OLS calibration — is that legitimate? <b>fire-haze</b> uses ROC-AUC on a 3.8% event
        class — is that the right metric?
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .hero { padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent); margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .hero h1 { max-width: 26ch; }
  .hero-actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.7rem; margin-top: 1.8rem; }
  .cta {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.82rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em; text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.05rem; border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground); background: var(--accent);
  }
  .cta.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--accent); }
  .cta { box-shadow: 0 0 28px -10px var(--glow); }
  .cta:hover {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 88%, var(--ink)); color: var(--ground);
    box-shadow: 0 0 34px -6px var(--glow);
  }
  .cta.ghost:hover { background: var(--panel); color: var(--accent); }

  .sec .col.wide { max-width: none; }

  .cards { list-style: none; margin: 1.5rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .card { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 1.4rem 0; }
  .card:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); }
  .card-head {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.9rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap; margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  .card-title {
    font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.18rem;
    text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink);
  }
  .card-title:hover { color: var(--accent); text-shadow: 0 0 22px var(--glow); }
  .card-title .arrow { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .card-title:hover .arrow { color: var(--accent); }
  .chip {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.64rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--flag); border: 1px solid currentColor;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.45rem; white-space: nowrap;
    text-shadow: 0 0 14px var(--glow-flag);
    box-shadow: 0 0 18px -12px var(--glow-flag);
  }
  .card-line { margin: 0 0 0.6rem; color: var(--muted); max-width: 62ch; }
  .card-found, .card-gap {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem; color: var(--muted); max-width: 74ch;
  }
  .card-found b, .card-gap b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .card-gap { color: var(--ink); }
  .card-gap b { color: var(--accent); }

  /* Role chooser: radios plus sibling selectors, no script. A visitor with JavaScript
     disabled still gets a working switch, and only the chosen panel is in the accessibility
     tree because the other is display:none rather than merely hidden visually. */
  .sr-only {
    position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; padding: 0; margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; border: 0;
  }
  .roles { border: 0; padding: 0; margin: 1.6rem 0 0; }
  .roles > input { position: absolute; opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }
  .roles > label {
    display: inline-block; cursor: pointer;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.82rem; font-weight: 500;
    padding: 0.55rem 1rem; margin: 0 0.5rem 0 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule); color: var(--muted);
    transition: color .15s ease, border-color .15s ease, box-shadow .15s ease;
  }
  .roles > label:hover { color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--accent); }
  .roles > input:checked + label {
    color: var(--ground); background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 26px -10px var(--glow);
  }
  .roles > input:focus-visible + label { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; }
  .panel { display: none; padding-top: 1.6rem; }
  #role-researcher:checked ~ .panel-researcher,
  #role-builder:checked ~ .panel-builder { display: block; }
  .panel-intro { color: var(--muted); max-width: 62ch; }
  .panel-next {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--muted); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 1rem; margin-top: 1.4rem;
  }
  .panel-next b { color: var(--ink); }
</style>
